                "reviews": [
                    {
                        "recommendationid": "237476872",
                        "author": {
                            "steamid": "76561198399600537",
                            "num_reviews": 2,
                            "playtime_forever": 282,
                            "playtime_last_two_weeks": 16,
                            "playtime_at_review": 189,
                            "last_played": 1792859808
                        },
                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "A John Butterfield design, very much influenced by the classic area-impulse system used in boardgames like Breakout: Normandy and Thunder at Cassino. While this is an earlier design than Drive on Moscow (using the same system but designed by Ted Raicer), it feels a lot more smooth in play. In particular, BoB feels a lot less \"sticky\" than DoM when undoing moves. It's probably a bit easier on the German player than its east front cousin, too.\r\n\r\nMy criticism of the system is the same as Drive on Moscow: the duration of an impulse should be more affected by the number and types of units moved during the impulse, as well as if those units engage in combat, and the size of said combat. It can still be random, that randomness should just be influenced by the actions you've taken.",
